" (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v30i2p8/pdf/)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 28; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/v30i2p8/pdf/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1168\nL0@P0: Combinatorial Derived Matroids\nL1@P0: Ragnar Freij-Hollanti\nL2@P0: Department of Mathematics and Systems Analysis\nL3@P0: Aalto University\nL4@P0: Helsinki, Finland\nL5@P0: ragnar.freij@aalto.fi\nL6@P0: Relinde Jurrius\nL7@P0: Faculty of Military Sciences\nL8@P0: Netherlands Defence Academy\nL9@P0: Den Helder, The Netherlands\nL10@P0: rpmj.jurrius@mindef.nl\nL11@P0: Olga Kuznetsova∗\nL12@P0: Department of Mathematics and Systems Analysis\nL13@P0: Aalto University\nL14@P0: Helsinki, Finland\nL15@P0: olga.kuznetsova@aalto.fi\nL16@P0: Submitted: Jun 14, 2022; Accepted: Mar 18, 2023; Published: Apr 7, 2023\nL17@P0: ©The authors. Released under the CC BY license (International 4.0).\nL18@P0: Abstract\nL19@P0: Let M be an arbitrary matroid with circuits C(M). We propose a definition of\nL20@P0: a derived matroid δM that has as its ground set C(M). Unlike previous attempts\nL21@P0: of such a definition, our definition applies to arbitrary matroids, and is completely\nL22@P0: combinatorial. We prove that the rank of δM is bounded from above by |M|−r(M)\nL23@P0: and that it is connected if and only if M is connected. We compute examples\nL24@P0: including the derived matroids of uniform matroids, the V´amos matroid and the\nL25@P0: graphical matroid M(K4). We formulate conjectures relating our construction to\nL26@P0: previous definitions of derived matroids.\nL27@P0: Mathematics Subject Classifications: 05B35\nL28@P0: 1 Introduction\nL29@P0: Let M be an arbitrary matroid. Gian-Carlo Rota [16] and Henry Crapo [6, 11] asked,\nL30@P0: partly independently and with various precise formulations, for a natural definition of a\nL31@P0: matroid δM that has as its ground set the collection of (co)circuits of M. Programmat\u0002ically, this would mean — among other things — a combinatorialization of notions such\nL32@P0: as syzygies and resolutions in algebraic geometry. For represented matroids, such a con\u0002struction is known [15, 10], but the combinatorics of δM is in general not uniquely defined\nL33@P0: ∗Supported by the Academy of Finland Grant 323416.\nL34@P0-1: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 https://doi.org/10.37236/11327by that of M, unless M is projectively unique [14], for example binary. The special case\nL35@P1: of binary matroids M was carefully studied by Longyear [12]. Another related notion is\nL36@P1: the lattice-theoretic notion of an adjoint geometric lattice to the lattice of flats of M [3],\nL37@P1: but this does also not exist for all matroids. Moreover, when an adjoint geometric lattice\nL38@P1: exists, it is not necessarily unique [1]. We propose a purely combinatorial construction of\nL39@P1: δM, defined via the rank function on M, and via an operation that resembles a closure\nL40@P1: operation on the collection of dependent sets.\nL41@P1: To our knowledge, the first actual definition in the literature of a derived matroid is by\nL42@P1: Longyear [12], in the case of binary matroids. However, these definitions are predated by\nL43@P1: predictions of their basic properties, made by Crapo [6], and more specific questions asked\nL44@P1: by Rota [16]. These predictions and questions, as well as some later work in the field,\nL45@P1: were formulated in two dual versions, with the ground set of the derived matroid being\nL46@P1: either the circuits or the cocircuits of the underlying matroid. For consistency, in this\nL47@P1: paper we reformulate all such results, whenever feasible, to the circuit-based definitions.\nL48@P1: We start by introducing the background on matroids and earlier work on derived\nL49@P1: matroids in Sections 2 and 3, respectively. In Section 4, we introduce our construction\nL50@P1: and give several cryptomorphic descriptions of it. We then give several examples of\nL51@P1: combinatorial derived matroids in Section 5. In Section 6 we show that the combinatorial\nL52@P1: derived matroid is connected if and only if the matroid is connected. In Section 7 we\nL53@P1: investigate the independent sets and rank of our construction, and in particular show that\nL54@P1: the rank is bounded from above by |M| − r(M). Section 8 is devoted to comparing our\nL55@P1: construction to the earlier definitions discussed in Section 3, and in particular deriving\nL56@P1: some conditions under which they are equal. Finally, in Section 9 we point out some\nL57@P1: directions for further research.\nL58@P1: 2 Preliminaries on matroids\nL59@P1: In this paper, we use several cryptomorphic definitions of matroids. Our construction of\nL60@P1: the derived matroid will start with a matroid defined in terms of circuits, and construct\nL61@P1: the derived matroid in terms of the dependent sets of the latter. We refer the reader to\nL62@P1: [13] for a reference on matroid theory.\nL63@P1: Definition 1 (Matroid). A matroid M is a pair (E, D) where E is a finite set and D a\nL64@P1: family of subsets of E satisfying\nL65@P1: (D1) ∅ ∈ D/ ;\nL66@P1: (D2) if D ∈ D and D ⊆ D0then D0 ∈ D;\nL67@P1: (D3) if D1, D2 ∈ D and D1 ∩ D2 ∈ D/ , then (D1 ∪ D2)\\{e} ∈ D for all e ∈ D1 ∩ D2.\nL68@P1: We call the members of D the dependent sets of M. The subsets of E that are not in\nL69@P1: D are thus independent. An inclusion-minimal dependent set is called a circuit, and the\nL70@P1: family C of circuits of a matroid M is characterized by the axioms\nL71@P1-2: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 2(C1) ∅ ∈ C / ;\nL72@P2: (C2) if C1, C2 ∈ C and C1 ⊆ C2 then C1 = C2;\nL73@P2: (C3) if C1, C2 ∈ C are distinct and e ∈ C1 ∩ C2, then there exists C3 ∈ C such that\nL74@P2: C3 ⊆ (C1 ∪ C2)\\{e}.\nL75@P2: Notation 2. Unless explicitly mentioned otherwise, E denotes a finite set, and its power\nL76@P2: set is denoted by 2E. The set of natural numbers N includes zero. If A and B are finite\nL77@P2: sets and F is a field, then F\nL78@P2: A×B is the set of matrices with rows indexed by A and columns\nL79@P2: indexed by B. We write F\nL80@P2: m×B as a shorthand for F{1,2,...,m}×B, and similarly FA×n as a\nL81@P2: shorthand for F\nL82@P2: A×{1,2,...,n}\nL83@P2: .\nL84@P2: Definition 3 (Rank and nullity). Let M = (E, D) be a matroid on the finite ground set\nL85@P2: E. The rank function r : 2E → N is defined by\nL86@P2: r(S) = max{|T| : T ⊆ S, T 6∈ D},\nL87@P2: and the nullity function n : 2E → N is defined by n(S) = |S| − r(S).\nL88@P2: It follows from property (D2) that rank and nullity are both increasing functions\nL89@P2: on 2E, and from (D3) that the rank function is submodular and the nullity function is\nL90@P2: supermodular, meaning that\nL91@P2: r(S) + r(T) > r(S ∩ T) + r(S ∪ T)\nL92@P2: and\nL93@P2: n(S) + n(T) 6 n(S ∩ T) + n(S ∪ T)\nL94@P2: for all S, T ⊆ E [13].\nL95@P2: Let R be a matrix with entries in the field F. The matroid associated to R is a\nL96@P2: matroid M(R) whose ground set is the set of columns of R and a subset of columns is\nL97@P2: said to independent if the corresponding submatrix is full-rank. A matroid M is called\nL98@P2: representable over the field F if there exists some R ∈ F\nL99@P2: m×E such that M(R) ∼= M. We\nL100@P2: sometimes refer to the row space of R as a code representing M.\nL101@P2: We will also need the notion of fundamental circuits with respect to a fixed basis B of\nL102@P2: M.\nL103@P2: Definition 4. Let B be a basis for M. For e ∈ E \\ B, the fundamental circuit CeB is the\nL104@P2: unique circuit contained in B ∪ {e}.\nL105@P2: Uniqueness follows as n(B ∪ {e}) = 1, and since B is independent it follows that\nL106@P2: e ∈ CeB.\nL107@P2: 3 Earlier definitions of derived matroid\nL108@P2: Several authors have investigated structures similar to the combinatorial derived matroid\nL109@P2: we are about to define. As explained before, the idea goes back to Crapo and Rota. We\nL110@P2: will describe four approaches to the question and explain how these are related.\nL111@P2-3: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 33.1 Longyear\nL112@P3: Longyear [12] introduced the notion of the derived matroid when M is a binary matroid.\nL113@P3: The primary motivation in [12] was to display the essential structure of a binary matroid\nL114@P3: in a computationally more tractable way. To do so, Longyear defined the notion of circuit\nL115@P3: bases, and correspondingly dependent sets of circuits, in terms of Kirkhoff sums, also\nL116@P3: known as iterated symmetric difference, and equivalent to binary sums. The Kirkhoff\nL117@P3: sum of the sets A1, . . . Ak is the set\nL118@P3: A1 ⊕ · · · ⊕ Ak =\nL119@P3: (\nL120@P3: x ∈\nL121@P3: [\nL122@P3: k\nL123@P3: i=1\nL124@P3: Ai: #{i : x ∈ Ai} is odd)\nL125@P3: .\nL126@P3: Definition 5. A circuit basis in a matroid M is a minimal collection A ⊆ C(M) such\nL127@P3: that every circuit in C(M) is the Kirkhoff sum of some subset of A.\nL128@P3: It follows from the definition that a set A ⊆ C(M) is dependent in δM precisely if it\nL129@P3: has a non-empty subset A0 ⊆ A such that the Kirkhoff sum of the circuits in A0is empty.\nL130@P3: In other words, A0 ⊆ A should be such that every element in E is be contained in an even\nL131@P3: number of the sets in A0\nL132@P3: . Longyear showed that under this definition, the derived matroid\nL133@P3: of a binary matroid is also a binary matroid. However, for a non-binary matroid, the set\nL134@P3: of circuit bases have no reason to constitute the set of basis for a matroid.\nL135@P3: Definition 6. Let M be a binary matroid. The matroid whose ground set is C(M) and\nL136@P3: whose bases are the circuit bases in M is called the Longyear derived matroid, and is\nL137@P3: denoted δL(M).\nL138@P3: 3.2 The Oxley–Wang definition\nL139@P3: A more general definition was introduced in [15] for represented matroids, which are ma\u0002troids along with a fixed representation. The derived matroid δM(R) of a representation\nL140@P3: R of the matroid M is determined by the generator matrix whose column vectors are the\nL141@P3: minimal support vectors in the dual of the given representation of M. By construction,\nL142@P3: such derived matroids are representable.\nL143@P3: Let M = (E, C) be a matroid on the finite ground set E with the set of circuits C. To\nL144@P3: motivate our further definitions, we first consider the case of a matroid M represented by\nL145@P3: a code Q ⊆ F\nL146@P3: n\nL147@P3: , with dual code Q⊥ ⊆ F\nL148@P3: n\nL149@P3: for some field F. As is commonplace in coding\nL150@P3: theory, for S ⊆ [n], we denote by Q|S the projection of Q to the coordinates in S, and by\nL151@P3: Q(S) the shortened code\nL152@P3: Q(S) = {q ∈ Q : supp(q) ⊆ S}.\nL153@P3: For a circuit C ∈ C(M), there is a codeword qC in Q⊥ with supp(qC) = C, and such a\nL154@P3: codeword is unique up to scalar multiple. We call qC a circuit vector of Q on C.\nL155@P3: Definition 7. Let Q ⊆ F\nL156@P3: n be a linear code, and let C be the collection of circuits of the\nL157@P3: associated matroid. Moreover, let A ∈ F\nL158@P3: n×C be the matrix that has a circuit vector qC in\nL159@P3-4: the electronic journal of combinatorics 30(2) (2023), #P2.8 4the column indexed by the circuit C ∈ C. We define the derived code δ(Q) to be the row\nL160@P4: span of A, and the Oxley–Wang derived matroid δOW (Q) to be the matroid represented\nL161@P4: by δ(Q).\nL162@P4: Clearly, if v ∈ Q⊥ is not a circuit vector, then v can be written as the sum of two\nL163@P4: vectors in Q⊥ whose support is strictly contained in supp v. Thus, Q⊥ is generated\nL164@P4: by its minimal support vectors, i.e. the circuit vectors. It immediately follows that\nL165@P4: the column space of the matrix A in Definition 7 is Q⊥, and so the rank of δOW is\nL166@P4: dim δ(Q) = r(A) = dim(Q⊥) = n − dim(Q).\nL167@P4: We can also present a family of explicit bases for δOW via fundamental circuits. For a\nL168@P4: fixed basis B and distinct elements e, f 6∈ B, we clearly have e 6∈ CfB. It follows that the\nL169@P4: collection\nL170@P4: δB = {CeB : e ∈ E \\ B}\nL171@P4: is independent in δOW . Since |δB| = |E \\ B| = n − dim Q, we see that δB is a basis for\nL172@P4: δOW (Q) for every basis B of M(Q).\nL173@P4: 3.3 Jurrius–Pellikaan\n"
